Requirements: 35   with obligations: 34   deliberately none: 1

[task-01] Obligation derivation issues one model call for the whole requirement
    registry. At scale that call sheds work: Gate 1 for #195 dropped 9 of roughly 36
    requirements in a single call at about 2.5k input tokens. That is DR-164's
    judgments-per-request failure, one stage earlier than the mapping stage where it was
    first found and fixed.
    -> obligation-partition-by-requirement-batch  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-03, constraint-01)
       Partition obligation derivation by requirement batch using the existing
       partitioning path, and make the batch size configurable from the command line as
       `--decompose-batch-size`.
    -> obligation-ceil-calls-per-batch  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-10, completion-02)
       Produce `ceil(N / size)` derivation calls for a task file with `N` requirements.
    -> obligation-dogfood-no-loss  [regression/explicit]   (also serves completion-10)
       Ensure a dogfood decompose run over a task file of #195's size loses no
       requirement.

[task-02] The same call also links. It may attach a requirement to an obligation derived
    from a different requirement, and that linking fails in both directions on one
    corpus under the same code, model and seed: seven scope exclusions declined with
    reasons that stated the obligation, then all five over-merged onto another
    requirement's obligation, with two Constraints absorbed into an obligation stating
    neither. Absorbed content is gone, and the run still reports every requirement as
    carrying a disposition, because that count reports dispositions rather than
    coverage.
    -> obligation-no-linking-in-derivation  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-03, constraint-06)
       Keep obligation derivation free of cross-requirement linking so a call may split
       one requirement into several obligations or decline it with `no_obligation`,
       while each obligation remains associated only with the requirement that produced
       it.
    -> obligation-obligation-id-unique-per-requirement  [invariant/explicit]   (also serves constraint-07)
       Within one derivation response, each obligation id appears in exactly one
       requirement's disposition.
    -> obligation-duplicate-obligation-id-logged  [error_handling/explicit]   (also serves constraint-08, completion-05)
       Record a response that names one obligation from two requirements through
       `UnusableAnswerLog`, and leave both affected requirements undisposed.
    -> obligation-every-requirement-has-disposition  [functional/explicit]   (also serves constraint-11, completion-03)
       Ensure every requirement carries a disposition after the merge.

[task-03] Partition obligation derivation by requirement batch, and stop derivation from
    linking at all.
    -> obligation-partition-by-requirement-batch  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-01, constraint-01)
       Partition obligation derivation by requirement batch using the existing
       partitioning path, and make the batch size configurable from the command line as
       `--decompose-batch-size`.
    -> obligation-no-linking-in-derivation  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-02, constraint-06)
       Keep obligation derivation free of cross-requirement linking so a call may split
       one requirement into several obligations or decline it with `no_obligation`,
       while each obligation remains associated only with the requirement that produced
       it.

[constraint-01] Obligation derivation is partitioned by requirement batch, through the
    existing `partition.py` rather than a second partitioning mechanism.
    -> obligation-partition-by-requirement-batch  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-01, task-03)
       Partition obligation derivation by requirement batch using the existing
       partitioning path, and make the batch size configurable from the command line as
       `--decompose-batch-size`.

[constraint-02] The whole task file appears in every derivation prompt. The batch scopes
    which requirements a call must answer for; it does not scope what the call may read.
    -> obligation-whole-task-file-in-prompt  [invariant/explicit]
       Include the whole task file in every derivation prompt while using the batch only
       to determine which requirements the call must answer for.

[constraint-03] `RunConfig.decompose_batch_size` exists and is settable from the command
    line as `--decompose-batch-size`.
    -> obligation-batch-size-configurable  [docs_config/explicit]
       Expose `RunConfig.decompose_batch_size` as a settable command-line option named
       `--decompose-batch-size`.

[constraint-04] The batch size is treated as a determinism control exactly as
    `mapping_batch_size` is: only the size enters the hashed request, and the batch
    index and batch count never do.
    -> obligation-batch-size-determinism-control  [invariant/explicit]   (also serves completion-06)
       Treat batch size as the only determinism control input for derivation request
       hashing, matching `mapping_batch_size`, so batch index and batch count do not
       affect the hashed request.

[constraint-05] A batch answers only for the requirements it was supplied. A returned
    requirement id that was not supplied to that call is recorded through
    `UnusableAnswerLog` rather than silently filtered.
    -> obligation-unsubmitted-requirements-logged  [error_handling/explicit]   (also serves completion-04)
       Record any returned requirement id that was not supplied to the call through
       `UnusableAnswerLog`.

[constraint-06] Obligation derivation performs no linking. A call may split one
    requirement into several obligations, or decline it with `no_obligation`, but it may
    not attach a requirement to an obligation derived from another requirement.
    -> obligation-no-linking-in-derivation  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-02, task-03)
       Keep obligation derivation free of cross-requirement linking so a call may split
       one requirement into several obligations or decline it with `no_obligation`,
       while each obligation remains associated only with the requirement that produced
       it.

[constraint-07] Within one derivation response, each obligation id appears in exactly
    one requirement's disposition.
    -> obligation-obligation-id-unique-per-requirement  [invariant/explicit]   (also serves task-02)
       Within one derivation response, each obligation id appears in exactly one
       requirement's disposition.

[constraint-08] A response naming one obligation from two requirements is recorded
    through `UnusableAnswerLog`, and neither affected requirement is treated as
    disposed.
    -> obligation-duplicate-obligation-id-logged  [error_handling/explicit]   (also serves task-02, completion-05)
       Record a response that names one obligation from two requirements through
       `UnusableAnswerLog`, and leave both affected requirements undisposed.

[constraint-09] Merged results are ordered deterministically, so batch composition and
    merge order are pure functions of the input.
    -> obligation-deterministic-merge-order  [invariant/explicit]
       Order merged results deterministically so batch composition and merge order are
       pure functions of the input.

[constraint-10] A task file with N requirements produces ceil(N / size) derivation
    calls.
    -> obligation-ceil-calls-per-batch  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-01, completion-02)
       Produce `ceil(N / size)` derivation calls for a task file with `N` requirements.

[constraint-11] Every requirement carries a disposition after the merge.
    -> obligation-every-requirement-has-disposition  [functional/explicit]   (also serves task-02, completion-03)
       Ensure every requirement carries a disposition after the merge.

[constraint-12] Changing the decompose batch size invalidates recorded transcripts.
    -> obligation-batch-size-invalidates-transcripts  [compatibility/explicit]
